[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Event Bubbling and Capturing in JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JavaScript provides two ways of event propagation: bubbling and capturing. These mechanisms determine the order in which event listeners are executed when an event occurs on an element that is nested within other elements."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Event Bubbling"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Event bubbling is the most common type of event propagation. When an event occurs on an element, the event listener on that element is executed first. Then, the event "bubbles up" to the parent element, and the event listener on the parent element is executed. This process continues up the DOM tree until it reaches the root element."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// HTML structure:
// <div id="outer">
//   <div id="inner">
//     <button id="button">Click Me</button>
//   </div>
// </div>

const button = document.getElementById('button');
const inner = document.getElementById('inner');
const outer = document.getElementById('outer');

button.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log('Button clicked');
});

inner.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log('Inner div clicked');
});

outer.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log('Outer div clicked');
});

// If you click the button, the output will be:
// Button clicked
// Inner div clicked
// Outer div clicked"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, when the button is clicked, the 'click' event listener on the button is executed first. Then, the event bubbles up to the inner div, and the 'click' event listener on the inner div is executed. Finally, the event bubbles up to the outer div, and the 'click' event listener on the outer div is executed."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Event Capturing"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Event capturing is the opposite of event bubbling. When an event occurs on an element, the event "trickles down" from the root element to the target element. The event listener on the root element is executed first, then the event listener on the next element down the DOM tree, and so on, until it reaches the target element."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// HTML structure:
// <div id="outer">
//   <div id="inner">
//     <button id="button">Click Me</button>
//   </div>
// </div>

const button = document.getElementById('button');
const inner = document.getElementById('inner');
const outer = document.getElementById('outer');

button.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log('Button clicked');
});

inner.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log('Inner div clicked');
}, true); // Note the 'true' for capturing phase

outer.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log('Outer div clicked');
}, true); // Note the 'true' for capturing phase

// If you click the button, the output will be:
// Outer div clicked
// Inner div clicked
// Button clicked"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use event capturing, you need to specify the 'useCapture' option as 'true' when adding the event listener. In the example above, the 'click' event listeners on the inner and outer divs are added with the 'useCapture' option set to 'true'. When the button is clicked, the 'click' event listener on the outer div is executed first, then the 'click' event listener on the inner div, and finally the 'click' event listener on the button."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Stopping Propagation"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can stop event propagation using the 'stopPropagation()' method of the event object. This method prevents the event from bubbling up or trickling down to other elements."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const button = document.getElementById('button');
const inner = document.getElementById('inner');

button.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log('Button clicked');
  event.stopPropagation(); // Stop the event from bubbling up
});

inner.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  console.log('Inner div clicked');
});

// If you click the button, the output will be:
// Button clicked
// (Inner div clicked will NOT be logged)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, when the button is clicked, the 'click' event listener on the button is executed. The 'stopPropagation()' method is called, which prevents the event from bubbling up to the inner div. Therefore, the 'click' event listener on the inner div is not executed."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Preventing Default Behavior"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you want to prevent the default behavior of an event. For example, you might want to prevent a link from navigating to a new page when it is clicked. You can prevent the default behavior of an event using the 'preventDefault()' method of the event object."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"const link = document.getElementById('myLink');

link.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  event.preventDefault(); // Prevent the link from navigating to a new page
  console.log('Link clicked, but navigation prevented.');
});"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, when the link is clicked, the 'click' event listener on the link is executed. The 'preventDefault()' method is called, which prevents the link from navigating to a new page. Instead, the message 'Link clicked, but navigation prevented.' is logged to the console."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Use Cases"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implementing complex UI interactions where the order of event handling matters."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creating custom form validation logic."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Building reusable components that need to control event propagation."}]
